**Definition 4.1 (finite structural compiler).** Use SSA Definition 5.2's
finite pattern enumeration for the union of the primitive bootstrap,
$D_0,L,D_1$ and the single Install instrument. Enumerate each finite
slot as absent/0/1; inspect the actual active version, mode, PC, cursor,
token and operand fields. Reject patterns inconsistent with the particular
native guard. Use SSA's byte, mark, latch and receipt effects, ordinary
EX convention, adjacent-cell routes, alias resolution and head retargeting.
For a fresh finite-bit WR use Definition 3.3 rather than an assignment to
an already existing switch. NEXT/ADVANCE update precisely their actual
existing mode/cursor fields; DONE alone changes the active table. An
ordinary new cell and a fresh finite control bit are different constructor
variants. Both physical IN bits have their actual latch-update patch.

All designated old target bits are assigned once after alias combination,
in first-role/track order, whether changed or unchanged. Construct core
cells before the receipt cell. For a fresh finite bit create its four
roles in the order $f,t,v_0,v_1$ before the receipt template. The new bit
is not also counted as an existing four-assignment target. Combine core
and receipt changes on a shared meter cell exactly as in SSA; its read
head is not inadvertently moved to the new append R.

Order table entries as SSA does, extended by absent before 0 before 1
and the displayed version/constructor order. Encode the guarded walks,
patterns, output labels, assignments and templates by SSA Definition 3.2's
unary/list encoding. Denote the resulting finite bit length by $d_\Pi$.
Thus both the rule table $\Pi$ and its description are specified by a
finite enumeration, not an unspecified graph interpreter.

